{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788216947,"nonce":"AoZbsKdkqW5rLWFIgVBGfBBvMRU=","message":"REMAINDER DONE — the gate-refusal early returns. Branch\n`issue/4087644-refusals-journal` (BORGES G2 worktree). Built Release clean, 0\nwarnings, 0 errors. NOT RUN IN GAME: workers launch nothing; phase 6 of\n`accept/4087644-order-honesty.md` is the exact call list for the orchestrator.\n\nTHE RULE I APPLIED, stated once so the four rulings are not four judgements\n\nAn early return is a REFUSAL — and therefore journals — when the verb RESOLVED\nITS TARGETS and then determined that no order could be issued. It owes nothing\nonly when it never reached a target: a bad argument, an empty pawn list, a\nthrow in argument resolution. That is exactly what `Outcome.Reached` already\nencodes (\"Reaching none of them is still no row\"), so this is not a new rule,\nit is the existing one applied at the sites that bypassed it by returning a\nhardcoded 0 instead of calling ActOn.\n\nThe acceptance-run comment asked whether `extinguish`'s `if (!burning)` is\ndifferent because \"nothing was attempted\". I ruled it is not, and the reason is\nthe cost comment #1 names rather than a definition of \"attempt\": an\n`extinguish` at a cell where nothing burns is a wasted order, the ledger is\nwhere wasted orders are counted, and \"which of my instructions were redundant\"\nis unanswerable if this class is silently absent. The line that DOES hold —\nrefusal vs never-reached-a-target — is the one already in the code.\n\nTHE FOUR RULINGS\n\n1. `extinguish` ~line 70, `if (!burning)` — REFUSAL, now journals.\n   The burning test is the provider's own gate:\n   FloatMenuOptionProvider_ExtinguishFires.GetSingleOption returns null when\n   `!TargetInfo.IsBurning`, so the game offers no option there. Arguments\n   resolved, a pawn list resolved, one wasted order. Gate `not-burning`,\n   recorded against the CELL.\n\n2. `beat-fire` ~line 180, `if (!onFire || fire == null)` — REFUSAL, journals.\n   FloatMenuOptionProvider_PutOutFireOnPawn requires a live Fire ATTACHMENT on\n   the clicked pawn; without one there is no option. Gate `not-burning`,\n   recorded against the TARGET via `Outcome.NoThing`.\n\n3. `tend` ~line 266, the ProviderGate refusal — REFUSAL, journals. THE PROVEN\n   ONE (your 5.1b). It already called `outcome.No(doctor, gate, reason)`, so\n   `Reached` was true and a row was owed, and then returned a hardcoded 0 —\n   every OTHER exit in that verb already routed through `TendRow`. Now it does\n   too. This is the single site where the defect was unambiguous before any\n   ruling was needed.\n\n4. `man-turret` ~line 464, `if (comp == null)` — REFUSAL, journals.\n   CompMannable.CompFloatMenuOptions is reached through\n   FloatMenuOptionProvider_FromThing, so a thing with no CompMannable is\n   offered no man option. Gate `not-mannable`, recorded against the TARGET.\n\nShape: each verb gained a `…Row` helper — the pattern `tend` and `repair`\nalready used — so the refusal exit and the normal exit emit one row definition\nrather than two that can drift. `Outcome` gained `NoAt(cell, gate, reason)`,\nthe cell-level sibling of the existing `NoThing`, so a verb whose target is a\nPOSITION can record one row per wasted ORDER instead of one per doer. That is\nthe only change to the shared substrate.\n\nTHE SWEEP — the whole verb surface, and it found five more\n\nGrepped every `Result(V, 0`, every `NoStamp()`, every hand-built `action`\nblock, and every journal emitter across all 60 files in `Source/AutoRimmer/`.\nEvery remaining instance of the shape is INSIDE my territory and is fixed:\n\n  PawnOrderVerbs.cs  equip   pawn.equipment == null      -\u003e EquipRow\n  PawnOrderVerbs.cs  equip   no CompEquippable           -\u003e EquipRow\n  PawnOrderVerbs.cs  wear    pawn.apparel == null        -\u003e WearRow\n  PawnOrderVerbs.cs  wear    thing is not Apparel        -\u003e WearRow\n  PawnOrderVerbs.cs  attack  CanDraftAttack refused      -\u003e AttackRow\n  PawnOrderVerbs.cs  move-to no standable cell           -\u003e GotoRow (NoAt)\n  PawnOrderVerbs.cs  prioritize, ALL FOUR refusal paths  -\u003e PrioritizeRow\n\nThe equip/wear four are the same defect as `tend`'s and were equally\nunambiguous: each recorded a verdict (`Outcome.No` / `Outcome.NoThing`, so\n`Reached` was already true) and then returned `outcome.Result(V, 0)`.\n\n`prioritize` is a different spelling of it and worth naming separately: it\nhand-builds its result rather than using `Outcome.Result`, and its four refusal\nexits published `NoStamp()` — literally `\"not applicable — nothing was\nmutated\"` over an order the game had just refused. A refused prioritize is\nprecisely a wasted order. It now carries an Outcome for the verdict only; the\nresult shape is unchanged apart from `action` becoming a real stamp and a\n`rejected` array appearing beside the existing `reason`.\n\nNOTHING WAS FOUND OUTSIDE MY TERRITORY. The other `NoStamp()` users —\nMedicalBillVerbs, PolicyVerbs, ResearchVerbs, SeekVerbs, PawnManageVerbs — were\nread and are correct as they stand: each is either a pre-target argument\nrejection or a genuine no-op query, not a refusal of a resolved order. If a\nlater spec disagrees for `surgery-add`/`policy-edit`, the rule above is the one\nto apply, not a blanket \"everything journals\".\n\n2.2b — THE SCRIPT WAS WRONG AND THE CODE WAS RIGHT\n\nAmended, and split so the reason is asserted beside the value: 2.2b now asserts\n`work_giver == null` and 2.2c asserts `ordered == false`. `PawnActs.JobFacts`\ncomputes `ordered = queuedNode \u0026\u0026 workGiver != null \u0026\u0026 forced` — the triple the\ndocument's own prose defines two paragraphs above its table — and a `wear`\norder has no WorkGiver. Fixed in BOTH the `.md` and the `.py` (line 346); the\n`.py` is technically outside the territory I was given, but leaving the\nrunnable driver asserting the opposite of its own prose twin would have been\nworse, so it is flagged here rather than skipped.\n\nON THE FIELD NAME, which you flagged for 4.1. I agree it invites the misreading\nand I did NOT rename it: 3.4's acceptance asserts on `ordered` by name and that\nis another spec's contract. What I recommend instead, in order of cost:\n\n  * NOW, and already true: the pair it is derived from travels beside it on\n    every job line — `player_forced` and `work_giver`. Anything answering \"did\n    my order take?\" reads those two, or the verb's own `action.journal_seq`,\n    never `ordered` alone. Written into the acceptance doc so the next reader\n    hits it.\n  * CHEAP, if 4.1 wants a checklist-safe field: publish `order_kind` beside it\n    — `\"work\"` when the triple holds, `\"direct\"` for a queued-node job with no\n    WorkGiver, `null` otherwise. It answers the question people actually ask of\n    `ordered` and needs no rename.\n  * A rename belongs in whichever spec next revises `pawn`'s state section,\n    with 3.4's script amended in the same commit. Doing it from here would\n    break another spec's acceptance silently.\n\n3.4's 5.12c: the `.py` was already amended to `ge(..., 1)`. The `.md` table row\nand the `.ps1` were NOT — both still asserted `journal_seq: null`, so a pwsh\nrun of that suite would have failed on this fix. All three twins now agree.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DEMONSTRATE\n\nEverything. No RimWorld instance was launched — nothing in this comment is a\nlive reading. Specifically not demonstrated: any of the four rulings firing on\nthe bench; the journal AGGREGATE showing the refused emergency orders; the\nequip/wear/attack/prioritize sweep sites; and `move-to`'s `no-standable-cell`\nrefusal, which additionally has no reliable fixture and is covered by\ninspection only. Phase 6A/6B of `accept/4087644-order-honesty.md` is the exact\ncall list, written to be fixture-free (a pawn is a Thing, so colonist B serves\nas the non-apparel / non-equippable / non-mannable target — deliberately not\nthe apparel, which phases 1 and 4 leave worn and therefore unspawned, where\n`ThingArg` would refuse it before the gate under test was reached).","files":null}]}